// DF-151i: a tuple holding a move-only element has NO `.copy()`, and says which
// element and why.
//
// The other half of giving the tuple its `.copy()`. Design 139's rule is that a
// wrapper carries the tier of what it wraps and offers the OPERATIONS that tier
// provides — so a tuple whose strongest element is ExplicitCopy or weaker gets
// the method (`df151i_tuple_copy.saw`), and a tuple holding a NoCopy element
// gets nothing, because the tier it carries provides nothing to give.
//
// The arm is gated exactly as the `Optional<T>` arm above it is (`copy_tier !=
// 'nocopy'`), so the two wrappers refuse in the same place for the same reason.
// The diagnostic names the OFFENDING ELEMENT by position and type rather than
// just the tuple: the tuple type as printed can be wide, and the element that
// withholds the method is the one thing the author has to change.
//
// The hint is checked here too, because it is the whole reason this finding
// existed — a refusal that recommends a spelling has to recommend one that
// works. `let (a, b) = move t` on a NoCopy-element tuple compiles and runs.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: is not Copy
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: element 0 of type `Res` is NoCopy
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: use `move` to transfer the tuple

struct Res {
    n: Int
}

extension Res: NoCopy {
    func deinit(&var self) { print("drop {self.n}") }
}

func main() {
    let t = (Res(n: 1), 5)
    let u = t.copy()
    print("{u.1}")
}
